      "A resume is not a list of everything you have ever done. It is a targeted argument for why you are right for a specific role. Every line should earn its place.",
      "Start with a short summary — two or three sentences about what you do and what you are looking for. Then your skills, grouped and specific. Then your projects and experience, with results.",
      "For Nigerian tech roles specifically: include your GitHub, your portfolio, your LinkedIn. If you have built something, link to it. Show, do not just tell.",
      "Tailor it. A resume for a frontend role should look different from one for a data role. Read the job description. Mirror the language they use.",
      "Finally, keep it to one page if you are early in your career. Hiring managers spend seconds on the first pass. Make those seconds count.",
      "Let's get concrete about structure, because formatting failures cost interviews. Header: name, phone (WhatsApp-enabled — recruiters here genuinely call and message), professional email, LinkedIn, GitHub, portfolio URL. Summary: two sentences naming your stack and the value you bring, written for the specific role. Skills: grouped honestly — languages, frameworks, tools — because Nigerian recruiters increasingly search CV databases by keyword like 'React', 'Power BI' or 'AWS', so exact names matter more than clever phrasing. Experience and projects: reverse chronological, each with an action verb, a technology, and ideally a number.",
      "That last part deserves unpacking: numbers make junior resumes credible even when you think you have none. 'Built a POS web app used by 3 shops in my estate' beats 'built a POS app'. 'Reduced report generation from 4 hours to 15 minutes by automating with Python' beats 'worked on reporting automation'. 'My capstone dashboard tracked 12,000 rows of sales data across 5 regions' tells a recruiter exactly what you can handle. You are not inventing scale; you are measuring what you actually did.",
      "Handle the Nigerian-specific realities deliberately. Many applicants have gaps or non-linear paths — NYSC years, family business stints, teaching jobs. Frame them honestly but usefully: those years taught stakeholder communication, operations, resilience; say so in one clause rather than hiding dates. If you are applying to banks or large corporates, expect ATS software and formal conventions: no photos, no tables that break parsing, standard section headings. Startups read CVs by hand and care mostly about your GitHub link working and your projects being interesting.",
      "Avoid the failure patterns we see constantly. Objective statements about 'seeking a challenging role in a reputable organization' — delete; nobody writes this for you specifically. Skill bars showing 80% JavaScript — meaningless and slightly embarrassing; list skills plainly. Listing every tutorial you completed — instead show what you built with it. And typos: in a market where attention to detail is the job, three spelling errors on one page is a rejection before the content is even read.",
      "Then treat tailoring as a ten-minute ritual per application. Read the job description twice, note the five requirements repeated most, and ensure your summary plus top bullets mirror that language where truthful. Save each tailored version clearly. It feels slower than spraying one generic PDF everywhere, but response rates tell the story: targeted applications outperform mass applications several times over, which means fewer hours per interview obtained, not more.",
