      "Product management is about understanding what users need, deciding what to build next, and making sure the team builds it well.",
      "The day-to-day work involves a lot of talking: to users to understand their problems, to engineers to understand what is possible, to stakeholders to align on priorities.",
      "For Nigerian tech companies, product management is still evolving. Many companies combine it with other roles. This is an opportunity.",
      "To prepare: practice identifying problems. Look at the apps and services you use daily. What frustrates you? What would you change? Write it down.",
      "At CEA, we expose learners to product thinking across all our tracks because understanding the why behind what you build makes you better at building it.",
      "Let's demystify a typical week. You will spend real hours with users — in Nigerian fintech that means watching agents at POS terminals, or interviewing market traders about how they actually record sales. You will write: specs, one-pagers, Slack updates that keep twenty people aligned without meetings. You will negotiate priorities constantly, because engineering capacity is always smaller than the idea list, and everyone from the CEO to customer support believes their request is urgent. And you will make judgment calls with incomplete data — ship now with known limitations, or delay for quality? There is no formula; there is only reasoning you can defend.",
      "The role varies enormously by company stage here. At startups of ten to thirty people, PMs often do everything: support tickets on Monday, pricing analysis Tuesday, user interviews Wednesday, roadmap deck Thursday. At banks and telcos, product work is heavier on compliance, stakeholder management across huge organisations, and squeezing incremental improvements through long release cycles. Neither is more 'real' product management — but knowing which environment suits you matters. If you love ambiguity and chaos, startup; if you like depth and scale, corporate.",
      "How do employers actually hire PMs in Nigeria? Rarely straight from certification courses alone. The credible signals are: evidence you have shipped something (your own side project counts), demonstrated user empathy (case studies where you identified a real problem and proposed solutions), analytical comfort (can you read a funnel and form a hypothesis?), and communication that survives contact with engineers. Many strong PMs transition internally — from support, operations, marketing or engineering — because they already know the customers and context. That path remains the most underrated entry route.",
      "Build the skill deliberately regardless of your current job. Write teardown analyses of products you use: why does this onboarding lose people? What would you test first? Practice writing crisp problem statements — 'market women in Port Harcourt need X because Y' — because vague problems produce vague roadmaps. Learn basic analytics: what a retention curve means, how to define an activation metric. Study pricing, since monetization decisions dominate Nigerian product conversations where willingness to pay is genuinely different from Silicon Valley assumptions.",
      "One honest warning: product management is not 'business strategy light' nor a escape hatch from technical difficulty. The best PMs I know carry enormous invisible load — being accountable for outcomes while controlling none of the resources, absorbing disappointment from every direction, staying constructive anyway. If that responsibility excites rather than frightens you, few roles teach you business faster. Start wherever you are: interview three users this week about anything, write up what you learned, and you have begun.",
