      "Be consistent. One conversation does not build a relationship. Follow up. Share an article. Congratulate them on their launch.",
      "At CEA, our community is one of our strongest assets. The relationships you build here will outlast any program.",
      "Let's make 'offer value' concrete, because that advice is useless until it has a form. Value at your level can be small and still real: share the article that explained closures to you with one clarifying thread; give genuine feedback on someone's side project; volunteer to help run registration at a meetup (organizers always need hands, and helpers become known); write the beginner tutorial you wish had existed. None of this requires seniority. It requires attention and follow-through, which are rarer than talent.",
      "Follow-up is where relationships actually form, and it has a simple mechanic: the next-day message. After meeting someone, send two sentences within 48 hours — reference something specific from your conversation, share one relevant link, end without an ask. Then maintain light touch monthly: congratulate their launch, comment substantively on their posts, introduce them to someone useful when you spot the connection. You are not managing contacts in a spreadsheet; you are being a person who follows through, which almost nobody does.",
      "Understand how referrals actually work here, because this is why networking pays. Most Nigerian tech roles get filled through warm introductions before or during posting — not as corruption, but because hiring is risky and trusted signals reduce risk. When a role opens, the hiring manager asks the team 'do you know anyone good?' Your goal is to be the name that comes up, which means being genuinely good AND being visible about it. Public work — open-source contributions, technical threads, conference lightning talks, portfolio case studies — makes you referable by people who barely know you.",
      "Events deserve tactical honesty too. Hackathons are among the highest-yield networking formats in Nigeria: you spend 24-48 hours building alongside people, which accelerates familiarity the way months of LinkedIn cannot. Conferences reward preparation — know the speaker list, have one thoughtful question ready, prioritize hallway conversations over collecting slides. And online counts equally: thoughtful comments on practitioners' posts over months create recognition that converts into DMs, calls and opportunities.",
      "Finally, play the long game with generosity. The junior developer you helped debug CSS today becomes the engineering manager hiring in four years. The peer learning alongside you joins a bank and remembers who studied with her. Nigerian tech is a smaller world than its size suggests — reputation compounds, both positively and negatively. Choose the boring virtues: show up, deliver what you promised, credit people publicly, help without invoicing. Ten years from now those habits will be the most valuable assets on your CV.",
